  ('client-side-open-redirect-evidence', 'generic', 'Broken access control', 'medium',
   'Client-side open redirect validation documented in bundle',
   'Login or OAuth flows validate ?next= redirects client-side via /api/auth/validate-redirect while URLSearchParams reads only the first duplicate parameter — HTTP-parameter pollution can bypass allow-lists.',
   'Post-auth redirects can send users to attacker-controlled URLs when validation inspects a different next= value than the one used for navigation.',
   'rule_ref',
   '{"rule":"clientSideOpenRedirectEvidence"}'::jsonb,
   'deep',
   '{"summary":"Validate redirect targets server-side with a single canonical next parameter and an allow-list of same-origin paths.","steps":["Reject protocol-relative URLs (//evil.com) and off-origin hosts in redirect handlers.","Parse only the last (or only) next= parameter server-side — never trust client-side validation alone.","Use signed redirect tokens instead of raw URL parameters after login."]}'::jsonb,
   'Our SPA uses /api/auth/validate-redirect with ?next= after login. Show how to enforce same-origin allow-lists server-side and prevent HPP bypasses.',
   true),
